        <h2 id="strengthening">Wrist Strengthening Exercises</h2>
        <p>These wrist exercises build the muscular strength that stabilizes your wrist under load. Many people do them 3 to 4 times per week after the warm-up, with at least one rest day between sessions.</p>

        <h3>5. Wrist Curls (Dumbbell)</h3>
        <p>Rest your forearm on your thigh with your palm facing up and wrist hanging off your knee. Curl the dumbbell up by flexing the wrist. Lower slowly through the full range of motion. This exercise strengthens the wrist flexors that maintain grip during dead hangs.</p>
        <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Detail</th><th scope="col">Recommendation</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Muscles</td><td>Flexor carpi radialis, flexor carpi ulnaris</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Sets × Reps</td><td>3 × 15 to 20</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Weight</td><td>Start with 3 to 8 lbs</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tempo</td><td>2 sec up, 3 sec down</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>

        <h3>6. Reverse Wrist Curls (Dumbbell)</h3>
        <p>Same position but with palm facing down. Extend the wrist upward against the dumbbell weight. Use a lighter weight than for wrist curls. The extensors are weaker. Extensor work is common in elbow rehab. It has not been shown to prevent tennis elbow.</p>
        <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Detail</th><th scope="col">Recommendation</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Muscles</td><td>Extensor carpi radialis, extensor digitorum</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Sets × Reps</td><td>3 × 15 to 20</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Weight</td><td>Start with 2 to 5 lbs</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tempo</td><td>2 sec up, 3 sec down</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>

        <h3>7. Radial &amp; Ulnar Deviation</h3>
        <p>Hold a dumbbell vertically (like a hammer) with your arm at your side. Tilt the dumbbell toward the thumb side (radial deviation) then toward the pinky side (ulnar deviation). This trains the side-to-side wrist muscles that stabilize during gripping and hanging.</p>
        <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Detail</th><th scope="col">Recommendation</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Muscles</td><td>Flexor/extensor carpi radialis, flexor/extensor carpi ulnaris</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Sets × Reps</td><td>2 × 12 each direction</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Weight</td><td>3 to 8 lbs (hammer position amplifies leverage)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>

        <h3>8. Pronation &amp; Supination</h3>
        <p>Hold a dumbbell in a hammer grip with your elbow bent 90 degrees. Rotate your forearm to turn the dumbbell from palm-up (supination) to palm-down (pronation). This trains the pronator teres and supinator muscles that rotate the forearm. These muscles can fatigue during long dead hang holds.</p>
        <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Detail</th><th scope="col">Recommendation</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Muscles</td><td>Pronator teres, supinator</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Sets × Reps</td><td>2 × 15 full rotations</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Weight</td><td>3 to 5 lbs</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>

        <h3>9. Rubber Band Finger Extensions</h3>
        <p>Wrap a rubber band around all five fingertips. Spread your fingers apart against the band's resistance. This trains the finger extensors that most grip programs neglect.</p>
        <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table">
