        <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>How do I strengthen weak wrists?</h3>
          <p>Strengthen weak wrists with wrist curls, reverse wrist curls and wrist circles 3 to 4 times per week, with a rest day between sessions. Start with a light weight and raise it slowly, about 1 lb every 2 weeks. This is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>What exercises help wrist pain?</h3>
          <p>Gentle movement is often comfortable for sore wrists. Wrist circles, prayer stretches, flexor stretches and extensor stretches are common choices, held for about 30 seconds each. Avoid loaded exercises until pain subsides. Rest and see a clinician if pain persists beyond two weeks.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Can wrist exercises prevent carpal tunnel?</h3>
          <p>The American Academy of Orthopaedic Surgeons publishes a home exercise program for carpal tunnel syndrome as one conservative option; splinting, activity changes and a clinician's review are others. No study shows wrist exercises prevent carpal tunnel syndrome.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>How often should I do wrist exercises?</h3>
          <p>Many people do wrist mobility exercises daily for about 5 minutes and wrist strengthening exercises 3 to 4 times per week, with at least one rest day between sessions. Avoid training wrist exercises to failure. Small tendons can be irritated by overuse.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Should I stretch my wrists before dead hangs?</h3>
          <p>Many people stretch their wrists before dead hangs. Wrist circles, prayer stretches and tabletop wrist rocks prepare the wrist for the sustained load of dead hanging. The warm-up takes about 3 minutes. Evidence that a warm-up prevents injury is limited. Save heavy strengthening exercises for after your dead hang session.</p>
        </div>

